\section{Choosing a method, which is not a detail}{

\code{"minmax"} is a linear rescale between the layer's own minimum and maximum.
It preserves the shape of the distribution, and it is \strong{extent-dependent}:
the same village scores differently depending on whether the biggest city in
the AOI is inside the frame. Fine for a single study area, wrong for
comparing two.

\code{"percentile_rank"} replaces each value by its rank among all cells. It
destroys the shape of the distribution and that is usually the point: with
population density, a handful of city cells otherwise compress everything
else into the bottom percent of a minmax scale. It is also extent-dependent.

\code{"log"} applies \code{log1p()} and then rescales, which keeps the ordering while
compressing a long right tail. It needs non-negative values, and it treats a
difference between 10 and 100 people as equal to one between 100 and 1000 —
a substantive claim about how vulnerability grows with exposure, not a
display choice.

None of the three is neutral. Whichever you use ends up in the provenance.
